  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Paper-and-Brass Telegraph",
    "overview": "CW 小站 is a compact, physical-feeling practice instrument: muted olive metal surrounds a warm printed paper strip, with a brass lever and dark rounded knob below it. The user’s committed identity is a retro metal telegraph machine with paper above the tactile key.\n\nMaterial changes carry the hierarchy. Paper holds language and progress; the metal deck holds reception, timing, and controls. Shadows describe weight, recesses, and mechanical movement. The interface stays quiet enough for repeated practice, with Chinese instructions beside condensed Latin instrument markings and monospaced code.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Olive metal, warm paper, and restrained brass.",
      "One centered instrument with paper above the tactile key.",
      "Mechanical depth and immediate press feedback.",
      "Distinct type roles for instructions, instrument labels, and telegrams."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Material Rule",
        "body": "Use paper for the message and metal for the operating controls; preserve their visibly different surfaces.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Instrument Type Rule",
        "body": "Use the condensed face for instrument markings, monospace for telegrams and Morse code, and system Chinese type for instructions.",
        "section": "typography"
